1|0@business^1439955829#cu7vwgn:weekendofsound$:&gt; I'm not deeming the job less important ... ...yet you said &gt; in a less than important industry But this isn't my point. I was born really poor, but I was lucky enough to have been born in a middle class community, and I went to a great elementary and high school in a state with some of the best public schools available. When I graduated high school, I tried to pay my way through community college working a part time job at a big box store, with the hope of doing a couple years there and then moving up. I couldn't. I didn't get paid enough to support myself, and my employer would either schedule me to work during class times, or they would cut my already minimal hours on class days instead of giving me different days off. When a close friend of mine got a position in HR, they told me that this was intentional. Here I was, trying to better myself, and my employer was undermining me so that I wouldn't be able to support myself and improve my lot in life. This is *common practice* at Walmart, and McDonalds, and Home Depot among others. So that is one thing. Once I gave up on school, I started moving around. I have lived and worked in some of the poorest parts of the country. These are people just like you and I - they want to do better for themselves and do well for their families and kids, but they didn't go to nice little public schools where they had SAT prep and favorable student/faculty ratios - they went to schools that were tasked with babysitting thousands of kids with learning disabilities and behavioral/emotional issues without adequate staff or training. While a handful of them can get scholarships to college because of this, it also means that the vast majority of these kids can't get the test scores, and they don't have the money to overcome that and actually learn a skill, so they end up in jobs like McDonalds or Walmart where they have little opportunity for advancement (and where any attempt to move ahead outside of work is undermined.) And then it becomes a circle - they grew up like this, they can't provide better for their kids, and then their kids grow up like that, and they can't provide better for their kids. So that is another. But finally, it is counterproductive to nitpick what our fellow workers make. You KNOW that pay is not a meritocracy- surely you have/had coworkers who got paid the same or more than you for doing lower quality work. Surely you are aware that there are entire careers where people make more money than you for doing less (I do) It is simply a waste of time and energy to concern yourself with this. The implication that a burger flipper is less skilled and less important than your position should only serve to tell you that your position deserves to earn more, not that you should stand in that burger flippers way to a better lot in life, *especially* because it gives you the opportunity to say that you are more valuable. In addition, I don't know where you live, but I presently live in Boston, which has a very similar (but still lower) cost of living than New York or LA, but I *can't imagine* how people get by on $10 per hour here. I pay $1400 for a one bedroom apartment, and that *is below market rate*. It is nice to think that only kids will take these shitty, low wage jobs and adults can all get better ones, but it's not factual. The service industry employs the most people these days - and *most* people aren't just high school students who need weed and gas money. To me, it doesn't matter how well they make a shitty burger - how much lipstick can you put on a pig? We need to make advancement an opportunity for everyone, not just those who were already born with opportunities. If it means I have to pay an extra few cents at checkout (and for a retailer like Walmart, it would be less than a quarter added to their average sale) I am okay with that, and I personally think that a lot of these people who are miserable and don't give a shit about how you don't want mayo or pickles probably would care a lot more if they didn't have to work 3 jobs to barely get by and not ever advance. 
