1|0@business^1439908120#cu71w1p:AndreTRON$:I get that. What I am saying here is that your business model is not adaptable, and that it has already failed. It has no flexibility to change with the times. If regulations require that you have to pay your employees more, and your business can't do that, then your business will have to either drastically adapt or cease operations. Neither of those options are a bad thing (for society, I see how its bad for you). Ok I need to acknowledge the extreme nature of my next comparison, but think about indentured servitude. Eventually, we got the the point where the government said ok this is fucked up, no more of this. We now need to make sure that we are paying everyone who is working (in the US Im pretty sure this was done by increasing the cost of transatlantic passenger travel). This regulation put a number of business out, but a few were able to adapt or change. In the end, these changes were necessary to bring about greater quality of life for the laboring population. Sure Indentured servitude worked out for a few people, but many didn't make it out of that lifestyle. How is this different from today? Many people aren't able to sustain themselves on jobs that pay less than $15/hr. Sure, there are a few who can work a min wage job and then work their way out to a sustainable lifestyle, but most can't. And that needs to stop. While yo may think you are helping many people who just need to "help pay for bills while in school, etc," you (your business) are in reality just perpetuating a system that unfairly disadvantages certain people. Look, the picture is MUCH MUCH bigger than this, but my point is that your business going under because you can't adapt is a good thing for society (in the long term), and yes a bad thing for you (in the short term). You, as a business owner will find a way to adapt because you have to. And if you can't, well at least you know that you can make $15 / hr somewhere. 
