-1|0@business^1439921654#cu7au59:AndreTRON$:Yes, exactly. Expect more of it. This is a good thing (again I'm talking about society here, not your personal interest). Businesses that depend on an underpaid labor force will suffer, and then close. This is what a failed business model looks like. Businesses like these are exploitative in nature, even though they may not be malicious about it. They exploit cheap labor to provide a cheap service or product, which keeps them in business. Businesses that can't operate without cheap labor deserve to close. When slavery was outlawed, a lot of plantations closed. Times were tough for a little while. People, and the economy recovered. Today, no one thinks this was a bad idea. When minimum wage is raised, a lot of businesses will close. Times will be tough for a while. People, and the economy will recover. Tomorrow, no one will think this was a bad idea. Now on the other hand, a business that formerly depended on cheap labor, but adapted to increased labor costs, changed their pricing structure, changed their product offerings - That is a business that deserves praise and support. Whether or not minimum wage goes up to $15 now, or goes up to $20 in the next 10 years, its going to happen eventually because of the unrest of those stuck in these kinds of jobs that can't support any sort of livable lifestyle. As a business owner, this is your challenge! What can you do to thrive in this new environment? Change! Adapt! I don't know! 
